{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Find evidence on economic impact: usage shares, cost/price differences, and consumer surplus of open vs closed AI models (including Nagle & Yue work)",
  "rationale": "The user specifically asks about economic impacts including consumer surplus estimates and the latent role of open models.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found extensive evidence: open-source models ~87-90% cheaper than closed models conditional on intelligence but have <30% market share (Demirer et al.); 5-29x cost reduction replacing GPT-4 with SLMs (Irugalbandara et al.); ~10x savings with fine-tuned open models (Wolfe et al.); stock market evidence showing open releases shift value to software (+1.28%) from compute (-0.91%) companies (Huang et al.); inference price half-life of 1.1 years for economy models (Du); Nagle & Yue cited but paper was unobtainable - referenced as studying the latent role of open models in AI economy.",
  "evaluation": "Comprehensive quantitative evidence gathered from multiple sources on costs, pricing, market shares, and stock market impacts. Nagle & Yue paper itself was unobtainable but is referenced in other works."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Find evidence on innovation and diffusion effects of open-source/open-weight AI models",
  "rationale": "The user asks about how open models affect innovation and technology diffusion compared to closed models.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found evidence that: 92% of AI models adopted in science are open-weight (Trisovic et al.); open models are customized 42% of the time vs 23% for closed; knowledge spillovers from downstream developers improve future model quality (Jamison & Yu); 10-11pp less likely to open-source per 10-point quality lead; Big Tech 20% more likely to open-source; Meta's open releases generated substantial research activity (Habibi); open models reduce downstream fine-tuning costs and create data flywheels (Xu et al.).",
  "evaluation": "Comprehensive evidence on innovation and diffusion effects gathered from multiple high-quality sources."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Find evidence on risk assessments, including the 2024 NTIA report on dual-use foundation models with widely available model weights",
  "rationale": "The user specifically references the NTIA report and asks about risk assessments of open models.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "NTIA recommended conditional, risk-based approach (not blanket ban) on open model weights. Benefits: cyber deterrence/defense, safety research, transparency. Risks: CBRN weapons access, offensive cyber operations, disinformation. Framework emphasizes marginal risk assessment. Kapoor et al. found existing research insufficient to characterize marginal risk. Bommasani et al. in Science found marginal risks uncertain and unevenly evidenced. Open models characterized as having larger attack surface but also defensive benefits.",
  "evaluation": "Detailed evidence gathered on NTIA findings and broader risk assessment frameworks from multiple authoritative sources."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Find evidence on trends in model openness by sector (academia, industry, government) and by country over time, including Epoch AI data and Stanford AI Index",
  "rationale": "The user asks about temporal and sectoral trends in model openness.",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Stanford AI Index 2025: Industry produced 90.2% of notable models in 2024, academia 0%. US led with 40 notable models, China 15, France 3. API access was most common (32.8%), followed by restricted open weights (18.03%), unreleased (16.39%), unrestricted open weights (11.48%). Open-weight model performance gap narrowed from 8.0% to 1.7% on Chatbot Arena. In science: 92% of adopted models are open-weight. China: 61% of open-weight selections in science from Chinese models by 2026. Global South API-only use rose from 0.4% to 10% of engaged papers.",
  "evaluation": "Comprehensive data gathered on sectoral and country trends from Stanford AI Index, Epoch AI data, and academic studies."
}"}

